00:02:45.843 --> 00:02:47.510
So if you haven't
used Cloud SQL before,

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.370
there are some
really great benefits

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:51.142
to using a managed database.

00:02:51.142 --> 00:02:53.350
The first of all is you
don't have to be DBA anymore.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.460
You can just let
Google take care of it.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.410
So it's fully managed.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:59.220
We have PostgreSQL
and MySQL instances.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.620
And you can run your
instances anywhere.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.560
So you can deploy to
any region in the world

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.275
that we support, and
it'll work great.

00:03:06.275 --> 00:03:08.150
You also get some
advantages like performance

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.300
and scalability.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.290
So Cloud SQL is
optimized for RVMs.

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:13.208
So we take MySQL.

00:03:13.208 --> 00:03:14.000
We take PostgreSQL.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.292
We tune all the flags so that
works as best as possible

00:03:16.292 --> 00:03:17.480
running on our hardware.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:19.850
And you can also change
the size of the hardware

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.300
with a few clicks of the button.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.042
So you can go from
one instance to 64.

00:03:23.042 --> 00:03:25.250
If you write an app and it
becomes popular overnight,

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.810
you can scale your database up.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.155
If you are running a lot
of high volume queries,

00:03:29.155 --> 00:03:30.680
and you need a little
bit of extra RAM instead

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.138
of a little extra CPU power so
that those have enough space

00:03:33.138 --> 00:03:36.420
to optimize, those options
are all available for you.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.660
And then last but not least, you
get reliability and security.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.680
So we've got 99.95 SLOs
around availability.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.550
And you also get advantages like
the high availability options,

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:50.025
which we're going to
talk about in a second.

00:03:50.025 --> 00:03:52.150
So high availability is
one of my favorite features

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:53.050
of Cloud SQL.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:55.300
Specifically for Postgres,
we have a really unique way

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.810
of implementing it.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.570
How it works is we have--

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.423
so in GCP, if you guys aren't
aware, we have regions.

00:04:01.423 --> 00:04:03.590
And then within those we
have fault tolerance zones.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.705
So zones are-- an
entire zone can go down

00:04:05.705 --> 00:04:07.330
and nothing will
affect all the others.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.090
They're completely separate.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:11.590
If you spin up a
Cloud SQL instance,

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:13.400
you start with a
primary instance.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.520
You're going to click one button
to enable high availability,

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.270
and you're going to
get a standby instance

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:18.087
in a second region.

00:04:18.087 --> 00:04:20.170
So that's going to work
using our persistent disk.

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:21.253
That's a service we offer.

00:04:21.253 --> 00:04:23.612
It does storage level
synchronous replication.

00:04:23.612 --> 00:04:25.570
So as soon as your database
writes in one zone,

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.550
it'll automatically
sync to the next zone.

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:28.630
The advantage of
this is you don't

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.100
have to worry about
any replication lag

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.017
or anything like that.

00:04:31.017 --> 00:04:33.660
As long as your database
accepted the transaction,

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.687
the backup is going
to have it as well.

00:04:35.687 --> 00:04:38.020
So because of this, we have
the replica waiting for you.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:40.380
All we have to do is say,
promote that one to master.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.130
Failovers are very, very quick--

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.665
typically take less
than 60 seconds.

00:04:44.665 --> 00:04:46.040
If you guys want
to check it out,

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.550
we have a demo on the floor
today, High Availability

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:49.660
with Cloud SQL.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.015
And the averages are
somewhere around 35 seconds.

00:04:54.707 --> 00:04:56.790
So connecting to Cloud
SQL-- when your database is

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.440
in a cloud, there's always
some interesting problems

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.760
you have to run into.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.420
So Cloud SQL has a few
different connection options.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.920
You pretty much have to choose
between two different groups.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.300
You have to choose
one connection path.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.175
And you have to choose
a way to authenticate.

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:11.092
So when it comes to
connecting with Cloud SQL,

00:05:11.092 --> 00:05:11.850
we have public IP.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:14.215
So that's an IP that's
open on the internet.

00:05:14.215 --> 00:05:16.090
You will have to pair
it with authentication.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.880
By default, it's closed off.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.890
You can only access it if
you're an approved user.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:22.900
And we'll go through how
to do that in a second.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.067
And we also have private IP.

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:25.860
So private networking
is available

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.750
if you're using
our VPC technology.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.510
That allows you to create
a cluster market as VPC,

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:32.490
and then all of the
things inside that VPC

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:33.770
talk directly to each other.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:35.640
They don't have to go
outside of Google's network.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:36.750
Everything stays private.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.810
Everything stays secure.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.847
With authentication, you've got
a couple of different options.

00:05:42.847 --> 00:05:44.430
Most of these are
used with public IP,

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:46.388
but they definitely can
be used with private IP

00:05:46.388 --> 00:05:48.010
if you want that extra security.

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:49.795
The first option is
white listing IP.

00:05:49.795 --> 00:05:52.170
You can create a list of IPs
that are allowed to connect.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.290
This is an OK option.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:54.900
But if any of you guys are
familiar with security,

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.900
there's a few disadvantages
to that, so we offer

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.442
some other options as well.

00:05:58.442 --> 00:06:00.150
You can have an approved
SSL certificate.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.340
So you can click a button,
get an SSL certificate,

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.710
distribute the client
key to your application,

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.068
and link up with it.

00:06:06.068 --> 00:06:08.610
Or you can use my favorite way,
which is the Cloud SQL proxy.

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:11.170
The Cloud SQL proxy has
a bunch of advantages,

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.000
including security.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.550
And it gives you control
with an IEM account.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.777
So you can actually
create an IEM account,

00:06:16.777 --> 00:06:18.610
say you're allowed to
talk to this database.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.910
And then, if for some reason you
decide that application should

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.440
no longer have access
to this database,

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.060
you can either remove
that privilege,

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.830
or you can just delete
that account entirely.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:27.330
And you'll note,
they will no longer

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:30.620
be able to access your
database, which is great.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.367
So today we're going to be using
the Cloud SQL proxy to connect.

00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:35.200
So like I mentioned,
it's really convenient.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:36.740
You don't have to worry
about white listing.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.740
You don't have to worry
about managing SSL keys.

00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:40.460
All you do is create
the IEM account,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.710
apply it to a service account,
and the service account

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:47.040
will connect on behalf
of your application.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.370
This makes it really
quick, really secure.

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.878
This proxy is also open sourced.

00:06:50.878 --> 00:06:51.920
It's available on GitHub.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.180
So if for some reason
there's something

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:54.710
with the proxy that's not
working for you right,

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:55.700
you can download the code.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.140
You can look at
it yourself, make

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:58.265
whatever edits you need to.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:02.880
OK.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.120
So with all that in
mind, let's go ahead

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:05.120
