00:08:44.702 --> 00:08:46.410
Let's go ahead and go
back to the slides.

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:48.618
So while that's going, let's
move on to the next step

00:08:48.618 --> 00:08:50.750
and talk about Kubernetes.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:53.970
So how many people have
used Kubernetes here before?

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:54.470
Great.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:57.000
Quite a few people.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.660
So at Google, we run
everything in containers--

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.340
as much stuff as possible.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.160
Gmail, web apps--
everything we can

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.710
runs in a container
on our infrastructure.

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:09.148
Even Google Cloud Platform
runs our VMs in containers.

00:09:09.148 --> 00:09:10.940
So whenever you spin
up a compute instance,

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.830
that's actually a container.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:15.600
And we launch several
billion containers per week.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.937
Now, obviously, Google
has a lot of employees,

00:09:17.937 --> 00:09:20.270
but not enough to hand-manage
several billion containers

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:20.770
a week.

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:23.510
That's a lot of
Docker runs to type.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.657
So we came up with
something called Kubernetes.

00:09:26.657 --> 00:09:28.490
So Kubernetes is actually
based on something

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:29.750
we have internally.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:30.878
It's open source.

00:09:30.878 --> 00:09:31.670
It's written in Go.

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:34.770
And what it does is it
manages fleets of containers.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.967
So what that means is that you
can essentially just tell it

00:09:37.967 --> 00:09:39.800
what you want your
application to look like,

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:41.660
and Kubernetes will
make it happen.

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:45.052
It is based on our internal
system, which is called Borg.

00:09:45.052 --> 00:09:47.510
We took a lot of the stuff we
learned when we created Borg,

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:49.413
and we brought it to Kubernetes.

00:09:49.413 --> 00:09:51.080
It supports multiple
cloud environments.

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:52.580
You can run this
not just in GCP.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:53.640
You can run it on Prem.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.250
You can run it on
whatever cloud you want.

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:57.083
And it supports multiple
container runtimes.

00:09:57.083 --> 00:09:58.580
So you're not
locked into anything

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:00.850
if you don't want to be.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:02.600
So if you aren't
familiar with Kubernetes,

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.990
we're going to go through
just a few terms that we use,

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:07.228
specifically, one of them--

00:10:07.228 --> 00:10:09.770
the first is, when it comes to
Kubernetes, you have clusters.

00:10:09.770 --> 00:10:11.270
So a cluster is
a group of nodes.

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:12.887
And there's one master node.

00:10:12.887 --> 00:10:14.720
So the master node
monitors the entire state

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:15.678
of all the other nodes.

00:10:15.678 --> 00:10:17.700
And it updates the
environment as needed.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:18.490
So it's going to take a look.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:19.990
It's going to say,
hey, I'm supposed

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:22.505
to have three nodes here,
but I'm missing one,

00:10:22.505 --> 00:10:23.630
or something's not working.

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.765
And it's going to
work to correct that.

00:10:25.765 --> 00:10:27.140
So when we talk
about node, we're

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:29.430
talking about a single
machine in a cluster.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:31.400
So we're going to
spin up a cluster.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:32.990
It's going to have
three nodes in it.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:35.750
And each node represents a
VM or a different machine

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:36.820
that we're running on.

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:39.320
And then last but not least,
we're going to talk about pods.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:40.970
So a pod is one or
more containers.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:43.250
So a pod is an atomical
unit in Kubernetes.

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:45.540
What it is, is it's an
instance of your application.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.540
So you try to make the
pod as small as possible.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:51.090
You represent one runtime
of your application.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:53.300
And sometimes, you have
multiple containers,

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:56.310
which we'll talk about as well.

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.340
So if you use Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:00.210
there's even a few
more advantages.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:01.310
So you have auto repair.

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.737
If one of your nodes goes
down-- so one of your VMs

00:11:03.737 --> 00:11:06.320
stops working for some reason,
whether it's something you did.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.270
It's something the
VM just crashed.

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:09.770
Sometimes that happens.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:10.910
We keep an eye on it.

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:13.220
And we automatically
repair that and replace it.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.108
So your node could go down.

00:11:15.108 --> 00:11:16.650
We'll put a new node
in your cluster.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.470
And you won't even notice.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:19.400
We have auto upgrade.

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:20.780
So you don't have to
worry about running

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:22.040
updates and stuff like that.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:24.290
You can just click a button,
and it will automatically

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:26.300
roll you up to the most
recent version of GKE.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:27.680
So if any of you
guys have ever tried

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:29.263
to manage a fleet
of your own servers,

00:11:29.263 --> 00:11:31.710
that is a super time saver.

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:33.920
And last but not least,
we even have auto scaling.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:36.253
We're going to talk a little
bit about pod auto scaling,

00:11:36.253 --> 00:11:37.815
but this is node auto scaling.

00:11:37.815 --> 00:11:39.440
So we can actually
add additional nodes

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:40.100
into your cluster.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.267
If your cluster is not big
enough to handle the load

00:11:42.267 --> 00:11:44.320
you're trying to, we can
throw in some extra VMs,

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:45.470
beef it up for a little while.

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:48.050
And then when the load subsides,
we can take those VMs back way.

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:49.842
And you only have to
pay for what you used.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:52.560
OK.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:54.480
So with all that in
line, let's go back

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:56.310
to the presentation--
or the computer

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:57.660
here-- and set up a GKE cluster.

00:12:01.868 --> 00:12:04.535
All right, so I'm going to click
right here to create a cluster.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:10.310
It's got a lot of
the same stuff.

00:12:16.127 --> 00:12:17.710
We'd better just
match numbers, right?

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:18.430
We don't want to be wrong.

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:18.700
OK.

00:12:18.700 --> 00:12:20.700
So we have a couple of
different location types.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.900
We can either have our
cluster be in a zone,

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:24.760
or we can have it be regional.

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.740
So what happens when
we select a region is

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.540
we no longer can select a zone.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:30.880
But instead, it's going
to create nodes inside

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:32.260
of each different region--

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.310
sorry-- each different zone.

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:36.130
So we're going to
have one region,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:39.620
but the nodes are going to be
spread throughout the zone.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:42.110
And we can also beef these
up a little, add some CPUs.

00:12:42.110 --> 00:12:42.610
Great.

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:45.070
So what's happening here is
I selected one for number

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:46.355
of nodes, but it's per zone.

00:12:46.355 --> 00:12:47.980
So I'm going to have
three nodes total,

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:50.063
and it's going to be spread
out between the zones.

00:12:54.220 --> 00:12:54.720
OK.

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:57.240
And if any of you have ever
tried to start up Kubernetes

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.700
before, this is 100 times
faster than I've ever spun it up

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:00.240
on my own.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:03.270
So I always love doing this,
because it's very fast.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:04.770
So while it's
spinning up those VMs,

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.320
we'll go back to the
slides a little bit.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:07.350
And then we'll come
back and take a look

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:08.308
these in a few minutes.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:15.010
Can we go back to the
presentation, please?

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:15.600
Great.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:16.100
OK.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:18.380
So let's talk about
deploying the application.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:20.813
Let's take a look
the infrastructure.

00:13:20.813 --> 00:13:23.230
So we have a couple of different
components going on here.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:25.840
It's a lot to take in,
but let's break it apart.

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:28.250
So the first thing we notice
is we have the end user.

00:13:28.250 --> 00:13:30.500
The end user is going to be
interacting through a load

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:31.010
balancer.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:33.010
The load balancer is
going to be distributing

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:34.540
all of the traffic
between the pods.

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:35.915
So we talked about
pods earlier--

00:13:35.915 --> 00:13:37.240
that's an atomical unit.

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:38.740
The pods are inside
a deployment.

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:40.157
So we're going to
tell Kubernetes,

00:13:40.157 --> 00:13:41.680
hey, we have a deployment.

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:43.960
The deployment is going
to manage the pods for us.

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:45.880
The load balancer is
going to direct traffic

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:47.307
to all of the pods.

00:13:47.307 --> 00:13:49.390
And the pods are going to
connect to our Cloud SQL

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:51.848
instance, and our Cloud SQL
instance has high availability.

00:13:51.848 --> 00:13:53.770
So it's got a
synchronous replication

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.223
to a standby instance.

00:13:56.223 --> 00:13:57.640
If we take a look
inside the pods,

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:00.760
you can see we have two
different containers-- one's

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.160
our application, and one's
using the Cloud SQL proxy.

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:06.400
So this is what we call
the sidecar pattern.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.650
So let's take a look inside
the application container.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:10.630
So this is a file from
the actual deployment.

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:12.840
You're going to see it in
a second, when I deploy it.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:14.257
You'll notice we're
using secrets.

00:14:14.257 --> 00:14:15.900
So secrets are a
way that Kubernetes

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:17.212
can represent information.

00:14:17.212 --> 00:14:19.170
You create a secret, and
it can interject those

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:21.220
into the pods as
environment variables.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:23.700
This is a really good way
to pass along information.

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:25.200
So here we're passing
in information

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.932
about the database user
and the database password.

00:14:27.932 --> 00:14:29.890
You can also see, if you
look on the top there,

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:31.020
we have the container port.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:32.367
So it's listening on 8080.

00:14:32.367 --> 00:14:33.825
This is just the
default for Flask.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:38.130
And you can see
for image, that's

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:41.520
going to pull container
image front right from my GCR

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.688
repository.

00:14:44.688 --> 00:14:45.730
We also have the sidecar.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:46.688
So this is the sidecar.

00:14:46.688 --> 00:14:48.350
It was an addition
to the main car.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.523
And you can see this
is the Cloud SQL proxy.

00:14:50.523 --> 00:14:51.690
This is an image we provide.

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:53.420
So you don't have to make
your own if you don't want to.

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:54.253
You can if you want.

00:14:54.253 --> 00:14:55.710
It's open source.

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.260
You can just slap this on there.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:58.652
It'll pull it down.

00:14:58.652 --> 00:15:01.110
And then you're just going to
give it the command to start.

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:02.900
So we're specifying
the instance name,

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:04.400
the port we want
the proxy to listen

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:07.130
to, and then the credential
file for the service account.

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:09.350
That's all we have to do
to get it up and running.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:11.810
We're using volume mounts
to pass this secret in.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:14.657
So it's mounting the
file as a volume.

00:15:14.657 --> 00:15:16.490
It's putting that volume
into the container.

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:18.800
And the container is accessing
it when it starts up.

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.410
So then we talked about
the load balancer.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:26.228
The load balancer-- all we're
going to do is specify a name.

00:15:26.228 --> 00:15:28.020
It's going to be called
tabs versus spaces.

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:29.820
We specify a type.

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:31.348
The whole thing is
called a service.

00:15:31.348 --> 00:15:32.640
We specify the type of service.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:34.410
The service is a load balancer.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:35.333
And then the selector.

00:15:35.333 --> 00:15:36.750
The selector is
going to determine

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:38.310
which pods it's pointing to.

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:40.980
So we're going to be using
app, tabs versus spaces.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:43.710
That says grab all of
the pods with the label

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:47.268
tabs versus spaces
and start using those.

00:15:47.268 --> 00:15:48.810
And then at the end,
we're specifying

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:50.130
what port we're listening on.

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.800
Now, just because my development
server listens on 8080

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.070
doesn't mean we want to here.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:15:55.410
So I'm going to
listen on port 80.

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:57.993
I'm going to send that traffic
to my pods, which are listening

00:15:57.993 --> 00:16:02.810
on port 8080 and with TCP.

00:16:02.810 --> 00:16:03.310
OK.

00:16:03.310 --> 00:16:05.435
So now let's walk through
deploying the application

00:16:05.435 --> 00:16:07.402
and take a look at everything.

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:16.277
If we can switch back to
the presentation, please.

00:16:16.277 --> 00:16:18.110
So now that we have
these up, let's go ahead

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:22.200
and take a look at what
exactly we have going on.

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:24.230
So this is the database
that we just created.

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:27.345
You can see here that
we see CPU utilization.

00:16:27.345 --> 00:16:28.970
So this is an instance
of the database,

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:30.970
but we actually have to
create the database real

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:31.730
quick on here.

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:33.560
You can do things like you
can manage connections.

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:34.470
You can manage users.

00:16:34.470 --> 00:16:36.260
All of that stuff
is provided here.

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:39.720
We've got backups, replications,
operations-- all that stuff.

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:41.660
I'm just going to create
a real quick database

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:45.440
for my application to
connect to and use.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:46.010
OK.

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:47.508
So that is creating.

00:16:47.508 --> 00:16:49.550
So now I can come over
here and do the same thing

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.290
with my GKE cluster.

00:16:51.290 --> 00:16:53.030
I can take a look.

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:54.470
You can see the master version.

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:55.340
It has an endpoint.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:56.840
So this is the
endpoint that I would

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:58.520
connect to the cluster itself.

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:00.552
We have a whole bunch
of other things in here.

00:17:00.552 --> 00:17:01.760
There's a lot of information.

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:03.230
It's all in here.

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:06.410
You can take a look at any
persistent volumes, any nodes.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:09.030
All of that stuff
is listed here.

00:17:09.030 --> 00:17:10.260
What I want to do is connect.

00:17:10.260 --> 00:17:12.813
So I'm going to go ahead and
click Connect right here.

00:17:12.813 --> 00:17:13.980
It's going to give me a URL.

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:16.942
And I'm going to take this
and move over to Cloud Shell.

00:17:16.942 --> 00:17:18.900
So if you guys haven't
used Cloud Shell before,

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:20.650
it's a really cool
thing that you get free

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:21.599
with the GCP account.

00:17:21.599 --> 00:17:24.839
Essentially what it
is is the VM that's--

00:17:24.839 --> 00:17:25.800
it's not persistent.

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:27.960
You keep your home
folder, but it launches up

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:29.200
with a click of a button.

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:31.037
So you can click it
and you can open it up.

00:17:31.037 --> 00:17:33.120
You can see right here I
have the application open

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:33.870
in the background.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:35.320
I haven't deployed it yet.

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:37.830
But just to show you guys
the code, it's very small.

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:40.160
It's one Python file.

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:45.170
It is 175 lines of code, and
most of that is comments.

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:47.720
So there's not a
lot going on there.

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:50.630
It's just going to connect
to the database for us.

00:17:50.630 --> 00:17:52.750
And if we put this--

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:54.420
OK, so that is the
command to connect.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:55.440
We're going to go
ahead and click that.

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.148
And it's going to
connect to our cluster.

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:02.640
OK, everybody read the
text OK, even in the back?

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:03.140
Good?

00:18:03.140 --> 00:18:04.760
Awesome.

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:05.420
All right.

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:05.920
OK.

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:10.070
So now I can talk to my cluster.

00:18:10.070 --> 00:18:12.260
OK.

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:12.760
Maybe.

00:18:16.360 --> 00:18:16.860
OK.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:17.810
No resources found.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:18.870
That's to be expected.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:20.630
Let's take a look
at the services.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:22.550
So we should see at least
one, because Kubernetes itself

00:18:22.550 --> 00:18:23.060
is a service.

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:23.390
OK.

00:18:23.390 --> 00:18:23.973
We've got one.

00:18:23.973 --> 00:18:25.520
So we're connected
to our cluster.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.360
Everything's good.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:29.090
So we talked a little
bit about secrets.

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:32.090
So let's take a look at
what the secrets looks like.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.020
We just have in here
the database name.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:35.122
Now these are encrypted.

00:18:35.122 --> 00:18:37.580
But you saw on the [INAUDIBLE]
earlier, it's not very hard.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:41.668
In case you forgot,
it's 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:18:41.668 --> 00:18:42.710
These are just encrypted.

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:44.870
You can either create
these via the command line,

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:47.780
or you can 64-bit encrypt
it and throw it in here,

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.630
and Kubernetes will take
care of it automatically.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.550
So I'm going to
