00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:40.150
So now that we've
deployed our application,

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:41.580
let's look at some of the
things that can go wrong

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.020
and what we can do to stop that.

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:44.770
We've already done a
lot just by deploying

00:23:44.770 --> 00:23:46.770
with Cloud SQL and Kubernetes.

00:23:46.770 --> 00:23:48.937
But let's look at
what else can happen.

00:23:48.937 --> 00:23:50.520
So when we talk about
fault tolerance,

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:53.310
it's important to remember
it's not fault proof, right?

00:23:53.310 --> 00:23:55.860
We don't want to try and make
sure that nothing can ever

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.030
go badly, ever.

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:58.208
That is a losing strategy.

00:23:58.208 --> 00:24:00.000
You will never be able
to make that happen.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.417
You're always going to have
some type of hardware failure,

00:24:02.417 --> 00:24:04.830
or something that's
impossible to schedule around.

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:07.710
What you can do is have a backup
plan for handling failures,

00:24:07.710 --> 00:24:10.380
so that if something does
go wrong, you react quickly,

00:24:10.380 --> 00:24:12.838
and hopefully, without a
lot of man hours involved.

00:24:12.838 --> 00:24:14.880
You don't want to focus
on eliminating the chance

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:19.542
to go down, focus on getting
back up as quickly as possible.

00:24:19.542 --> 00:24:21.250
So let's talk about
application failures.

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:23.100
Now, this is like
170 lines of Python.

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:26.220
I'm not very good at Python,
so maybe I did something badly,

00:24:26.220 --> 00:24:28.380
and somebody figures out
a way to make it crash.

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:29.460
What's going to happen?

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:31.320
So Kubernetes is
constantly monitoring this.

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:33.570
It's going to take a look
and say, hey, wait a minute.

00:24:33.570 --> 00:24:35.040
There's supposed to
be a pod running,

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:36.720
and it's supposed to have
this application in it.

00:24:36.720 --> 00:24:37.950
I don't see any pods.

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:40.200
It's going to start that
pod back up within seconds.

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:40.830
I can go in.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:41.640
I can kill a pod.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:45.200
Kubernetes is going to
bring it right back up.

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:46.080
High traffic.

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:47.780
So let's say that
my little pod wasn't

00:24:47.780 --> 00:24:51.350
enough to handle all of the
500-plus people in this room.

00:24:51.350 --> 00:24:53.670
If that were the case, we
have a feature we can do.

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:55.670
We can add a pod autoscaler.

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:59.250
It's going to add more pods
as the CPU usage increases.

00:24:59.250 --> 00:25:01.950
So as more people begin to go
ahead and use the application,

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:04.760
Kubernetes is going to go, hey,
this one pod is not keeping up.

00:25:04.760 --> 00:25:06.663
I need to throw
some more in there.

00:25:06.663 --> 00:25:07.830
I don't have to do anything.

00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:09.000
It's automatically
going to react.

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:09.740
It's going to scale up.

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:11.323
It's going to handle
that load for me.

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:13.850
OK.

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:15.030
How about
infrastructure failure?

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:16.370
So now we've talked
about a bunch of things

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:17.240
that can go wrong on my end.

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:19.032
What happens if an
entire zone goes down?

00:25:19.032 --> 00:25:20.240
What happens if it wipes out?

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.170
I can't talk to
that node anymore.

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:24.020
I can't talk to my
Cloud SQL instance.

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:25.580
Well, we made a
bunch of decisions

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:27.230
earlier, when we decided
to deploy regionally,

00:25:27.230 --> 00:25:29.510
that even if a zone goes down--
remember the zones are fault

00:25:29.510 --> 00:25:30.520
tolerant in different ways.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:33.080
So if one go zone goes down,
the others are still remaining.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:36.530
So our Cloud SQL instance
is going to failover.

00:25:36.530 --> 00:25:38.658
We're going to see it go
down for maybe a minute,

00:25:38.658 --> 00:25:39.950
and it's going to come back up.

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:41.800
And our Cloud-- our
Kubernetes instance

00:25:41.800 --> 00:25:43.050
is going to do the same thing.

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:44.425
It's going to look
in the cluster

00:25:44.425 --> 00:25:45.770
and go, whoa, hold on a second.

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:47.330
There's supposed to be
a bunch of pods running.

00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:48.500
I don't see anything.

00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:50.420
We need to fix that.

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:51.320
Again, hands off.

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:53.940
I don't have to do anything.

00:25:53.940 --> 00:25:55.940
So with that in mind,
let's take a second

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:57.874
and examine the fault tolerance.

00:26:03.503 --> 00:26:05.670
Actually, let's look at the
vote real quick and see.

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:09.550
OK.

00:26:09.550 --> 00:26:11.140
Somebody has automated tabs.

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:13.400
And as you can
clearly see, spaces

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:15.160
is not going to catch
up anytime soon.

00:26:19.090 --> 00:26:19.590
All right.

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:23.320
OK.

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:25.150
So if we look, we've
got two pods running.

00:26:25.150 --> 00:26:26.960
That pod seems to
be doing OK so far.

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:28.460
But we have this
load balancer here.

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:31.160
So what exactly is this
load balancer going to do?

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:32.630
It's pretty simple.

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:35.140
It's going to apply
to all the labels

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:36.730
where tabs versus spaces is.

00:26:36.730 --> 00:26:40.382
So it's going to look at all the
pods with tabs versus spaces.

00:26:40.382 --> 00:26:41.590
Oh, that's the load balancer.

00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:44.260
I want the autoscaler, sorry.

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:44.780
Autoscaler.

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.350
So this is horizontal
pod autoscaler.

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:48.760
We have minReplicas right here.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:50.950
So minReplicas says, hey,
I want at least two pods

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:52.190
running all the time.

00:26:52.190 --> 00:26:53.560
So what that means is
that even if something

00:26:53.560 --> 00:26:54.970
goes wrong in that
pod, there may

00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:57.280
be a split second where
Kubernetes doesn't

00:26:57.280 --> 00:26:58.322
realize that pod is down.

00:26:58.322 --> 00:27:00.947
But as long as I have two, even
if something goes wrong in one,

00:27:00.947 --> 00:27:02.570
the other one is
still up and running.

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:04.510
So even if we're waiting a
couple of seconds for a new pod

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:06.850
to come up and replace it,
I have that one, at least,

00:27:06.850 --> 00:27:09.070
responding to requests.

00:27:09.070 --> 00:27:10.298
I can also set a max, right?

00:27:10.298 --> 00:27:12.340
So I want to make sure I
don't run too many pods.

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:14.230
If I only have three
nodes, too many pods

00:27:14.230 --> 00:27:15.647
isn't going to
help me out either.

00:27:15.647 --> 00:27:17.210
It's going to add
a lot of overhead.

00:27:17.210 --> 00:27:18.460
So I want to limit that to 10.

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:20.470
So only have 10 pods.

00:27:20.470 --> 00:27:23.390
Anywhere between 2
and 10 is perfect.

00:27:23.390 --> 00:27:25.730
We can also look at the
target's CPU utilization.

00:27:25.730 --> 00:27:28.180
So this is how Kubernetes
is deciding to scale.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:30.850
This is a metric that-- you
can create your own metrics.

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:32.590
Kubernetes is really extensible.

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:34.180
This essentially
says, hey, I want

00:27:34.180 --> 00:27:35.963
each pod to be about 50% load.

00:27:35.963 --> 00:27:37.630
That's going to give
me a little leeway,

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:39.970
just in case the pod
spikes up in traffic.

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:41.950
It's going to go, hey,
this pod's way over 50%.

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:43.700
I'm going to throw
some more pods in there

00:27:43.700 --> 00:27:44.980
to balance it out.

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:47.470
And then we just have some
API stuff right at the bottom

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:50.020
that you don't really
have to worry about.

00:27:53.120 --> 00:27:57.500
So with that in mind, let's
deploy my auto balancer--

00:27:57.500 --> 00:28:00.330
or my autoscaler.

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:00.830
OK.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:02.570
So now it's creating
my autoscaler.

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:05.260
It takes it a little
bit to start up.

00:28:05.260 --> 00:28:08.920
I can check the
status of it with HPA.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:11.500
So this shows my--

00:28:11.500 --> 00:28:14.020
HPA is the Horizontal
Pod Autoscaler.

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:15.820
It's going to show
its status right here.

00:28:15.820 --> 00:28:17.253
Right now, I'm
unknown out of 50%.

00:28:17.253 --> 00:28:19.420
So it's still trying to
initialize, get some things,

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:20.630
see what's going on.

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:22.402
We see minpods, 2; maxpods, 10.

00:28:22.402 --> 00:28:24.360
So let's look and see if
our pods have changed.

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:30.110
I still only have one pod.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:31.800
So the HPA is taking
its sweet time.

00:28:34.610 --> 00:28:35.575
I see replicas one.

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:39.080
OK.

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:39.580
Sweet.

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:41.183
So now it's started
up a second pod.

00:28:41.183 --> 00:28:42.350
It's only eight seconds old.

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:43.993
So it's still getting there.

00:28:43.993 --> 00:28:46.410
But we've got our backup pod
in case something goes wrong.

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:48.118
So even if I were to
crash one of these--

00:28:56.465 --> 00:28:58.445
SPEAKER 1: Any chance
you could bump that up?

00:28:58.445 --> 00:28:59.930
KURTIS VAN GENT: Yes.

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:01.917
SPEAKER 1: Not font size.

00:29:01.917 --> 00:29:03.250
KURTIS VAN GENT: Is that better?

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:15.545
Is it still big enough
for everybody to read?

00:29:15.545 --> 00:29:18.708
SPEAKER 2: It's not the font.

00:29:18.708 --> 00:29:20.000
SPEAKER 3: They want it higher.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:22.837
You should offset
the bottom line.

00:29:22.837 --> 00:29:23.670
KURTIS VAN GENT: Oh.

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:24.740
I see.

00:29:24.740 --> 00:29:25.240
Sorry.

00:29:25.240 --> 00:29:26.650
I totally misunderstood.

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:35.150
SPEAKER 1: There you go.

00:29:35.150 --> 00:29:36.483
KURTIS VAN GENT: Is that better?

00:29:36.483 --> 00:29:37.600
All right.

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:38.100
Thank you.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:38.520
Thank you.

00:29:38.520 --> 00:29:39.020
Thank you.

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:41.250
I'll be here all day.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:41.750
OK.

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:43.010
So I deleted that
pod, and now you

00:29:43.010 --> 00:29:44.030
can't really notice,
because I took

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:46.910
too long to figure out what you
guys were trying to tell me.

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:51.238
So it's hard to tell, but
this pod was with the b7mv8.

00:29:51.238 --> 00:29:52.530
And that pod is no longer here.

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:54.430
So let me do this one more time.

00:29:58.565 --> 00:30:00.190
So if anybody is
still trying to vote--

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:03.610
which I'm sure the guy who
automated that tab script is--

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:05.830
he's probably not seeing
any dropped connections.

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:07.380
Maybe one or two,
if he's unlucky.

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:08.620
But as soon as that
pod gets dropped,

00:30:08.620 --> 00:30:09.730
Kubernetes realizes it.

00:30:09.730 --> 00:30:12.860
It stops directing traffic to
it and sends it the other way.

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:14.020
So if I go right back--

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:19.410
OK.

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:20.160
I've got two pods.

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:21.420
One's only 16 seconds old.

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:24.990
So the one that I killed
has already been replaced.

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:28.600
So let's check again.

00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:30.493
All right, tabs
is really winning.

00:30:30.493 --> 00:30:32.160
Somebody on spaces
is trying to keep up,

00:30:32.160 --> 00:30:34.368
but tabs must just be better
at automating the votes.

00:30:37.090 --> 00:30:37.590
OK.

00:30:37.590 --> 00:30:39.410
So we also have that autoscaler.

00:30:39.410 --> 00:30:42.280
So let me start up some traffic.

00:30:42.280 --> 00:30:45.650
So I've created a
compute instance here.

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:47.990
I'm going to grab my IP
address if I can find it.

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:55.080
I'm going to grab
the IP address.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:57.340
And you guys are just
not quite enough load.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:58.200
So let's see what
would happen if we

00:30:58.200 --> 00:30:59.340
had several thousand
people trying

00:30:59.340 --> 00:31:00.630
to log into this all at once.

00:31:07.960 --> 00:31:08.460
OK.

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:11.002
So what this is doing is it's
just sending a ton of requests.

00:31:11.002 --> 00:31:14.040
It's using a tester called hey.

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:15.210
It's written in Go.

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:16.710
It's just sending
a ton of requests,

00:31:16.710 --> 00:31:19.120
trying to max out that
as much as possible.

00:31:19.120 --> 00:31:23.670
And we can even come
in here and monitor it.

00:31:23.670 --> 00:31:27.845
So we can take a look at our
service, tabs versus spaces.

00:31:27.845 --> 00:31:29.220
So you can see
right now, there's

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:31.067
a sharp upcrease in CPU usage.

00:31:31.067 --> 00:31:33.150
That's because we're sending
it a lot more traffic

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:35.475
than we were.

00:31:35.475 --> 00:31:38.010
But so far we still
just have the two pods.

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:43.870
OK.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:44.960
So while that's
going, we're also

00:31:44.960 --> 00:31:46.460
going to talk about
the database failover.

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:48.560
So it's going to take like
maybe 30 or 40 seconds

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:51.320
for Kubernetes to figure
out that it needs more pods.

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:53.172
The autoscaler is customizable.

00:31:53.172 --> 00:31:55.130
We're just using the
default options right now,

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:57.570
so it plays a little
on the safe side.

00:31:57.570 --> 00:32:01.173
We're going to
switch the database.

00:32:01.173 --> 00:32:03.090
And there is a button
right here for failover.

00:32:03.090 --> 00:32:04.470
So this is really
useful if you want

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:05.850
to test how your
application works.

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:06.820
You can click failover.

00:32:06.820 --> 00:32:08.610
It's going to simulate
that failover process.

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:10.610
So it's essentially going
to crash your database

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:11.473
intentionally.

00:32:11.473 --> 00:32:13.890
Your data will be fine, but
it will allow your application

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:16.435
to practice failing over.

00:32:16.435 --> 00:32:17.810
So we're going to
click failover.

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:19.643
It wants to make sure
I'm really this crazy.

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:24.820
OK, great.

00:32:24.820 --> 00:32:28.470
So now the application is
failing, and it's down.

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:30.200
So if I take a look--

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:32.550
it's not working, right?

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:33.050
Sorry.

00:32:33.050 --> 00:32:34.310
The application is
working, but it's

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:36.727
getting an error because it
can't connect to the database.

00:32:36.727 --> 00:32:38.600
So whoever is voting your app--

00:32:38.600 --> 00:32:41.168
or your script just crashed.

00:32:41.168 --> 00:32:42.960
And it's going to be
down for a little bit.

00:32:42.960 --> 00:32:45.030
It's going to take
like 30 or 40 seconds.

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:47.370
And while we're waiting
for that to react,

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:50.720
let's go ahead in here
and really screw it up.

00:32:50.720 --> 00:32:53.120
I'm going to delete
all of the pods.

00:32:59.933 --> 00:33:01.600
So unfortunately, I
don't have the power

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:04.450
to bring a zone
down all on my own.

00:33:04.450 --> 00:33:06.835
But I'm going to delete
all the pods, because it's

00:33:06.835 --> 00:33:07.960
a similar situation, right?

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:10.150
I've already shown you
that I can delete a pod

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:12.040
and it's going to
come right back up.

00:33:17.884 --> 00:33:18.384
Maybe.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:25.700
OK.

00:33:25.700 --> 00:33:26.080
Perfect.

00:33:26.080 --> 00:33:27.400
So now it's deleting
all the pods.

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:29.150
You can actually see
it deleted four pods.

00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:31.330
So the autoscaler
was working hard.

00:33:31.330 --> 00:33:34.340
It kicked up some extra pods,
and now it's deleted those.

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:39.180
So if I take a look
inside Kubernetes--

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:40.140
if I refresh this--

00:33:42.860 --> 00:33:43.360
OK.

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:44.897
You see here, I
actually caught one

00:33:44.897 --> 00:33:47.230
of the pods that's in the
process of being switched out.

00:33:47.230 --> 00:33:48.790
So we have four healthy
pods that have already

00:33:48.790 --> 00:33:49.998
replaced the four that broke.

00:33:49.998 --> 00:33:52.780
And we have one that's
crashed and running.

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:56.250
And hopefully, if we
take a look over here,

00:33:56.250 --> 00:34:02.332
my database is back up,
and so is my application.

00:34:02.332 --> 00:34:03.540
So you guys were watching me.

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:04.700
I wasn't doing anything.

00:34:04.700 --> 00:34:06.630
Kubernetes took care
of that all on its own,

00:34:06.630 --> 00:34:08.713
with a little help from
Cloud SQL on the back end.

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:14.010
Can we go back to
the presentation?

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:15.887
So that is examining
fault tolerance.

00:34:15.887 --> 00:34:17.429
We have about 15
minutes left, so I'm

00:34:17.429 --> 00:34:19.830
going to do some
questions at the end here.

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:22.929
If you guys are interested
in trying this yourself,

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:24.870
we have a code lab
that's available online.

00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:26.695
All the code for a
similar application.

00:34:26.695 --> 00:34:28.320
You can create your
own meme generator.

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.278
If the only thing that's
different between tabs

00:34:30.278 --> 00:34:31.889
versus spaces,
it's making memes.

00:34:31.889 --> 00:34:34.320
So you can go to
this link right here,

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:37.409
GitHub.com/Googl
eCloudPlatform/gmemegen.

00:34:37.409 --> 00:34:39.150
It's a hands-on,
step-by-step tutorial.

00:34:39.150 --> 00:34:40.840
It does exactly
what I just did now,

00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:42.632
so you can go home and
try it out yourself.

00:34:42.632 --> 00:34:45.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

